\section{Computational evidence}

This paper contains no computational experiments and no machine-checked proofs; all five results are argued by hand in Section~3. The purpose of this appendix is to list, for each theorem, where the corresponding source material may be consulted and under which label the statement and its argument are filed. Those labels are provenance marks, not mathematical content.

\begin{itemize}
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:sylow1} corresponds to the recorded statement \texttt{stmt:000060} (a theorem of Sylow type), whose recorded proof is the elementary argument reproduced in Section~3: coset counting, the orbit--stabilizer theorem, the class equation, the fixed-point congruence, Cauchy's theorem and an iterated extension step.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:sylow2} corresponds to \texttt{stmt:000061}, whose recorded proof is the action of a Sylow $p$-subgroup on a coset space together with the fixed-point congruence.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:sylow3} corresponds to \texttt{stmt:000062}, whose recorded proof is the conjugation action on the set of Sylow subgroups combined with the fixed-point count applied to a Sylow subgroup.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:index} corresponds to \texttt{stmt:000063}, the index form $n_p=[G:\Norm_G(P)]$ of the counting statement.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} corresponds to \texttt{stmt:000064}, the criterion that a Sylow subgroup is unique exactly when it is normal.
\end{itemize}

Sources: the recorded statements carry the literature identifiers \texttt{lit:000000} (Conrad) for the first four and \texttt{lit:000002} (Idelhaj) for the uniqueness criterion; the paper's bibliography lists the same four sources as \cite{conrad,harris,idelhaj,vakil}. No page numbers are cited because none were available verbatim in the project reference collection.

